---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import {
  ArrowRight,
  BellRing,
  Bot,
  Brain,
  Check,
  CirclePause,
  Clock3,
  Eye,
  FileSearch,
  GitFork,
  Inbox,
  Layers,
  MessagesSquare,
  ShieldCheck,
  Sparkles,
  Stethoscope,
  Workflow
} from "lucide-react";
import agentsCapture from "../assets/agents.png";
import artifactsCapture from "../assets/artifacts.png";
import chatCapture from "../assets/chat.png";
import doctorCapture from "../assets/doctor.png";
import guardrailsCapture from "../assets/guardrails.png";
import inboxCapture from "../assets/inbox.png";
import knowledgeCapture from "../assets/knowledge.png";
import memoryCapture from "../assets/memory.png";
import notificationRulesCapture from "../assets/notification-rules.png";
import triggersCapture from "../assets/triggers.png";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { releaseFacts } from "../data/release";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../data/site";

/*
 * WHY SEVERAL CLAIMS ON THIS PAGE ARE NARROWER THAN THEY WERE.
 *
 * Every feature bullet below was read against the RELEASED tag this site publishes, and the
 * ones that only held on core's development branch were narrowed or cut. Recorded here in
 * the frontmatter rather than beside the markup on purpose: an Astro template comment is
 * emitted into the HTML, and a retracted claim quoted verbatim in the shipped page is a
 * string a reader — or a scraper — can still find and believe.
 *
 * CUT ENTIRELY — the safety-profile bullet. `guardrails/policy.py` is a closed island at
 * this release: `SafetyProfile`, `profile_for_session`, `tool_grants`, `tool_allowlist` and
 * `egress_tier` have no reference outside that module, its re-export and its own test file,
 * and the gateway still picks between auto-approve and hook-based approval in exactly the
 * branch the module's own docstring claims to have replaced. It was wired a week AFTER the
 * tag, so it is a development-branch feature. /compare's `tool_approval` row carries the
 * same finding, including the correction that the profile is as inert as the two list
 * fields it already disclosed.
 *
 * GUARDRAILS, narrowed. The incident kill switch reaches four automation paths — cron,
 * event triggers, script hooks, subagent spawn — not "every automation": the module names
 * seven seams and three are unwired. The app's own guardrails panel already listed the
 * four, so this page was the only surface overstating it. Budgets are enforced per DAY; the
 * per-run limit is a settable control nothing reads, and a per-run DOLLAR ceiling has never
 * existed in any version. The three prompt-scanning modes are real but do not cover the
 * native tool-calling path.
 *
 * DOCTOR. "Read-only" now says "dashboard Doctor", which earns it — sqlite opened ro, and
 * probes that decline the mutating helpers. The CLI `personalclaw doctor` rewrites
 * `personalclaw.json` unprompted when it finds a fixable defect, so the unqualified
 * sentence was false about the command a reader is most likely to type.
 *
 * MEMORY. Procedural moved out of the RECALL list. All three layers are written, but
 * procedural is a write-only sink here: its single reader has no production caller and
 * procedural rows are explicitly excluded from the injection query. Correction learning and
 * fact promotion are both real, with live callers and a visible surface.
 *
 * STACK. "SQLite state" was the wrong shape, not a wrong word — the project's own
 * durability inventory is five SQLite entries against twenty-one trees, eighteen JSON files
 * and five JSONL logs. Chat history is JSONL and config is JSON; SQLite holds memory,
 * knowledge, the lexicon and loops.
 *
 * TRIGGERS. There is no webhook trigger KIND at this release — the trigger id parser
 * accepts schedule, lifecycle and event only. What exists is a Bearer-authenticated inbound
 * endpoint that runs an agent turn, wired with curl rather than picked from a list.
 *
 * ARTIFACTS. Previews really render for widgets, HTML, React, documents, SVG and images,
 * but office and video types are routed down the image path and show a broken glyph — so
 * "not placeholder tiles" was true of the types named and false of the rest. Dedup matches
 * on NAME: there is no content hash anywhere in the artifacts package.
 *
 * CHAT. The memory mode is chosen when a session is created and frozen after the first
 * turn; no route changes it later and the control disappears once a chat starts.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Product · PersonalClaw"
  description="Explore PersonalClaw's agentic chat, autonomous goal loops, memory, knowledge, automation, and agent runtimes."
>
  <section class="product-hero">
    <Image
      class="hero-image"
      src={chatCapture}
      alt="PersonalClaw agentic chat with conversation history, tools, and context"
      widths={[800, 1200, 1600]}
      sizes="100vw"
      format="webp"
      quality={88}
      loading="eager"
    />
    <div class="shell hero-overlay">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The product</p>
        <h1 class="display">The whole system, under one roof.</h1>
        <p>
          Move from conversation to autonomous work, durable context, and
          scheduled action without splitting your personal operating layer
          across five services.
        </p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href="#chat">
          Start the tour <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section product-promise section-rule">
    <div class="shell promise-grid">
      <div class="promise-statement">
        <p class="kicker">Designed as a system</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Every mode of work shares the same context and controls.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="promise-points">
        <p>
          Chat, goal loops, and automation use the same context engine,
          approvals, skills, apps, and durable state.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><Check size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> One local gateway</li>
          <li><Check size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> One web dashboard</li>
          <li><Check size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> One inspectable home directory</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tour section-rule" id="tour">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="tour-head">
        <p class="kicker">The 84-second tour</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">One unedited run through the parts that matter.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="tour-support">
        A single recording of {releaseFacts.core.versionLabel} on one machine — the
        version in the capture's status bar is the release this site publishes. Chat
        against a local model, the approval it stops on, a goal loop, the local corpus,
        and the file the agent actually wrote. No narration, no cuts, nothing sped up.
      </p>
      <!-- No autoplay: nothing else on this site plays media on load, and an 84-second
           capture that starts itself is a worse first impression than a good still. The
           wrapper reserves 16/9 so the poster, the first decoded frame and a failed load
           all occupy the same box — this section contributes no layout shift.

           preload="none", not the handoff's "metadata". Chromium serves "metadata" by
           range-requesting the header and ABORTING the rest, and
           tests/browser/support.ts asserts zero failed network requests on every route.
           What "metadata" buys is a scrubber that knows its duration before first play,
           and the heading and copy both state the length in words. So this section costs
           the poster and nothing else until a visitor opts in. -->
      <div class="tour-frame">
        <video
          class="tour-video"
          width="1280"
          height="720"
          controls
          muted
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          poster="/media/personalclaw-tour-poster.webp"
        >
          <source src="/media/personalclaw-tour.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          <track
            default
            kind="captions"
            srclang="en"
            label="English (described beats)"
            src="/media/personalclaw-tour.vtt"
          />
        </video>
      </div>
      <ol class="tour-beats" aria-label="What the tour shows, in order">
        <li><span class="index">01</span> Chat against a model on this machine</li>
        <li><span class="index">02</span> The approval it stops on before writing</li>
        <li><span class="index">03</span> A goal loop with its findings</li>
        <li><span class="index">04</span> Knowledge searched locally</li>
        <li><span class="index">05</span> The artifact it wrote, on disk</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section feature-section" id="chat">
    <div class="shell feature-grid">
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <span class="feature-icon"><MessagesSquare size={23} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
        <p class="kicker">Agentic chat</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Conversation that can become work.</h2>
        <p>
          Run tools with approval controls, organize sessions, branch from
          earlier turns, compare answer variants, and tune model or memory
          behavior per conversation.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Session fork and undo</li>
          <li>Folders, tags, and kanban</li>
          <li>Temporary and incognito memory modes, set when a chat starts</li>
          <li>Per-session model overrides</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-media">
        <Image
          src={chatCapture}
          alt="PersonalClaw chat session with tools and conversation controls"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 62vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={86}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="autonomy-band">
    <div class="shell autonomy-grid">
      <div class="autonomy-title">
        <p class="kicker">Autonomous goals</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">The agent keeps going. You keep the final say.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="autonomy-points">
        <article>
          <Eye size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Observe</h3>
          <p>See the plan, active cycle, tool activity, and result as it happens.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <CirclePause size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Intervene</h3>
          <p>Pause, nudge, resume, or stop without losing the accumulated run state.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <ShieldCheck size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Bound</h3>
          <p>Deterministic supervisors and approval modes constrain autonomous action.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section context-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Durable context</p>
          <h2>Memory and knowledge do different jobs.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          PersonalClaw keeps learned personal context separate from the source
          material you deliberately add. Both remain inspectable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="context-grid">
        <article>
          <div class="context-copy">
            <Brain size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
            <div>
              <h3>Memory learns how you work.</h3>
              <p>
                Semantic and episodic layers support recall, correction learning,
                and promotion of repeated facts. A procedural layer records
                tool-outcome priors alongside them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <Image
            src={memoryCapture}
            alt="PersonalClaw memory view with layered learned context"
            widths={[520, 760, 980]}
            sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 50vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={84}
          />
        </article>
        <article>
          <div class="context-copy">
            <FileSearch size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
            <div>
              <h3>Knowledge keeps the source.</h3>
              <p>
                Ingest documents, web pages, and media; enrich them with
                entities and make them searchable from chat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <Image
            src={knowledgeCapture}
            alt="PersonalClaw knowledge base with source documents and entity graph"
            widths={[520, 760, 980]}
            sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 50vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={84}
          />
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section automation-section section-rule">
    <div class="shell automation-grid">
      <div class="automation-media">
        <Image
          src={triggersCapture}
          alt="PersonalClaw automation triggers with schedules and status"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 62vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={86}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <span class="feature-icon"><Clock3 size={23} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
        <p class="kicker">Automation</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Let the system notice first.</h2>
        <p>
          Schedule recurring work, react to webhooks, monitor connected
          channels, and dispatch background subagents without building a second
          automation stack. Anything that needs you lands in one inbox you can
          answer in place &mdash; a loop waiting on a decision, a proposed
          skill, an approval you walked away from.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Cron and interval triggers, plus an inbound webhook endpoint</li>
          <li>One inbox for decisions, approvals, and messages</li>
          <li>Delivery per kind: notify, badge, digest, or never</li>
          <li>Background subagents</li>
          <li>Automatically matched workflow SOPs</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section context-section section-rule">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">One place to answer</p>
          <h2>Work that waits on you, in one queue.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          A loop needing a decision, a proposed skill, an approval you walked
          away from &mdash; these used to be toasts that scrolled past while the
          work stayed stalled. Now they are items you answer in place.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="context-grid">
        <article>
          <div class="context-copy">
            <Inbox size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
            <div>
              <h3>Everything waiting, together.</h3>
              <p>
                Decisions, proposals, approvals, and messages share one inbox.
                Each row deep-links to the loop or chat it came from.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <Image
            src={inboxCapture}
            alt="PersonalClaw inbox showing loop decisions, proposals, approvals, and messages"
            widths={[520, 760, 980]}
            sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 50vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={84}
          />
        </article>
        <article>
          <div class="context-copy">
            <BellRing size={23} aria-hidden="true" />
            <div>
              <h3>You choose what interrupts.</h3>
              <p>
                Per kind of notification: notify, badge, batch into a daily
                digest, or never. Quietening one noisy kind no longer raises the
                bar for everything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <Image
            src={notificationRulesCapture}
            alt="PersonalClaw per-kind notification delivery rules"
            widths={[520, 760, 980]}
            sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 50vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={84}
          />
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section automation-section section-rule">
    <div class="shell automation-grid">
      <div class="automation-media">
        <Image
          src={artifactsCapture}
          alt="PersonalClaw artifacts library with live previews and collections"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 62vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={86}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <span class="feature-icon"><Layers size={23} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
        <p class="kicker">What it produced</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Output you can find again.</h2>
        <p>
          Documents, spreadsheets, charts, and diagrams the agent made stay in a
          library with live previews &mdash; searchable, versioned, and grouped
          into collections instead of scattered through a chat log.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Live previews for widgets, documents, SVG and images</li>
          <li>Version history and diffs between revisions</li>
          <li>Collections, and name collisions caught at save time</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section agents-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="agents-copy">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Bring the right agent</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Native, ACP, or app-defined.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            PersonalClaw can run its native agent or connect external coding
            agents through ACP. Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, and Kiro CLI remain
            behind the same host approval boundary.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/apps">
            Explore agent apps <ArrowRight size={17} aria-hidden="true" />
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Image
        src={agentsCapture}
        alt="PersonalClaw agents view showing configured agent runtimes"
        widths={[720, 1080, 1440]}
        sizes="100vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={86}
      />
      <div class="agent-proof" aria-label="Agent runtime properties">
        <span><Bot size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> Multiple runtimes</span>
        <span><GitFork size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> ACP boundary</span>
        <span><Workflow size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> Shared context</span>
        <span><Sparkles size={18} aria-hidden="true" /> Replaceable providers</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section automation-section section-rule">
    <div class="shell automation-grid">
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <span class="feature-icon"><ShieldCheck size={23} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
        <p class="kicker">Runs safely on its own</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Autonomy with a floor under it.</h2>
        <p>
          Before you hand the system unattended work, it gives you the controls
          to trust it: a kill switch that halts scheduled runs, triggers, script
          hooks and subagent spawns at once, daily spend ceilings, outbound
          secret scanning, and per-provider circuit breakers. Interactive chat is
          never affected.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Incident kill switch, explicit resume</li>
          <li>Token and dollar budgets per day</li>
          <li>Outbound prompt scanning on agentic calls: warn, redact, or block</li>
          <li>Per-provider circuit breakers, with provider health on view</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="automation-media">
        <Image
          src={guardrailsCapture}
          alt="PersonalClaw Guardrails settings with the incident kill switch, daily spend budgets, and outbound scan mode"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 62vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={86}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section automation-section section-rule">
    <div class="shell automation-grid">
      <div class="automation-media">
        <Image
          src={doctorCapture}
          alt="PersonalClaw Doctor showing tiered health probes across every subsystem, all systems healthy"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 62vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={86}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <span class="feature-icon"><Stethoscope size={23} aria-hidden="true" /></span>
        <p class="kicker">Diagnoses itself</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">See what's healthy, and fix what isn't.</h2>
        <p>
          The dashboard Doctor runs read-only health probes across every
          subsystem — memory, channels, local models, app backends, the SPA,
          model-provider breakers. A degraded capability is never mistaken for a
          dead gateway, confirm-gated fixes repair the common faults, and a
          health-scored engine keeps the stores tidy on its own.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Tiered probes with per-capability evidence</li>
          <li>No-model degraded mode, stated plainly</li>
          <li>Confirm-gated repairs and crash capture</li>
          <li>Health-scored self-maintenance</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section product-cta">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="kicker">Open source · self-hosted</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Run the system on your machine.</h2>
      <p>
        Python 3.12, an aiohttp gateway, React dashboard, state on disk as SQLite
        plus plain JSON and JSONL, and no product telemetry.
      </p>
      <div>
        <a
          class="button button-primary"
          href={GITHUB_URL}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Get PersonalClaw <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/security">
          Read the security model
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .product-hero {
    position: relative;
    height: min(780px, calc(100dvh - 112px));
    min-height: 620px;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--night-soft);
  }

  .product-hero::after {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(11, 12, 13, 0.99) 0%, rgba(11, 12, 13, 0.92) 34%, rgba(11, 12, 13, 0.2) 76%),
      linear-gradient(0deg, var(--night) 0%, transparent 42%);
    content: "";
  }

  .product-hero :global(.hero-image) {
    position: absolute;
    width: min(1500px, 94vw);
    height: auto;
    top: 7%;
    right: -9%;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 8px;
    box-shadow: 0 40px 100px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
  }

  .hero-overlay {
    display: grid;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    height: 100%;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .hero-overlay > div {
    max-width: 690px;
  }

  h1 {
    max-width: 11ch;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .hero-overlay p:not(.kicker) {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .promise-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.2fr 0.8fr;
    gap: clamp(3rem, 10vw, 10rem);
  }

  .promise-statement h2,
  .feature-copy h2,
  .autonomy-title h2,
  .agents-copy h2,
  .product-cta h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .promise-statement h2 {
    max-width: 13ch;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .promise-points > p,
  .tour-support {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
  }

  .tour-head h2 {
    max-width: 26ch;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .tour-support {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  /* The 16/9 box is reserved here, not by the video, so the poster and the decoded
     frames land in the same place and this section shifts nothing. */
  .tour-frame {
    max-width: 1080px;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 0 35px 90px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.42);
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  }

  .tour-video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .tour-beats {
    display: grid;
    max-width: 1080px;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 0;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    list-style: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .tour-beats li {
    display: flex;
    min-height: 48px;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem 0.9rem 0;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .tour-beats .index {
    color: var(--coral);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-mono);
  }

  .promise-points ul,
  .feature-list {
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .promise-points li {
    display: flex;
    min-height: 48px;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    align-items: center;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .promise-points svg {
    color: var(--success);
  }

  .feature-grid,
  .automation-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(280px, 0.68fr) minmax(600px, 1.32fr);
    gap: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .feature-section,
  .automation-section {
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .feature-icon {
    display: grid;
    width: 48px;
    height: 48px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    place-items: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 50%;
